Based on my research:

**Setup scaffold** — The existing 5.3.31 scaffold already pulls `spring-aspects`, `spring-context`, and `spring-tx` all pinned to the tested version, plus AssertJ. Spring-aspects' AOP behavior (transaction/cache/async infrastructure aspects, `@Configurable` bean configuration) is implemented via **compile-time-woven** AspectJ ITDs baked into the spring-aspects jar itself (`aspectOf()` static-aspect pattern) — no load-time weaving, no runtime service (DB/broker/cache backend), nothing that needs a container. The test class only exercises in-memory constructs (`ConcurrentMapCacheManager`, `SyncTaskExecutor`, a hand-rolled `PlatformTransactionManager`). Spring-aspects 6.0.0's transitive `aspectjweaver:1.9.9.1` is already a tested, metadata-covered artifact in this repo. I found no evidence of a needed extra dependency, Docker image, or system property.

**Unsupportable check** — The issue's log is truncated right after one test passes (`genericInterfaceDrivenAspectConfiguresConfigurableObjects() SUCCESSFUL`) and cuts off before showing the actual failing exception/stack trace. Per the required process, step 1 (FAILURE) can't be completed from the evidence given, so every later step is moot — this must default to repairable, not `skip_unsupported`.

